// What the screens say about each weekly portion of Numbers (Bamidbar). The layout of each entry is
// explained at the top of lessons-genesis.js. Verse numbers follow the Hebrew Bible (as Sefaria does);
// in chapters 16–17, 25–26 and 29–30 they differ from English Bibles.
window.TORAH_LESSONS = window.TORAH_LESSONS || {};
Object.assign(window.TORAH_LESSONS, {

  'Bamidbar': {
    meaning: 'In the wilderness',
    summary: 'A year after leaving Egypt, God tells Moses to count the Israelites, tribe by tribe. The tribes camp in a set arrangement around the Mishkan, three on each side, with the Levites in the middle. The Levites are counted separately, take the place of the firstborn, and are given the job of carrying the parts of the Mishkan.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Everyone counts', text: 'God counts the people again and again. Rashi says it’s because they’re precious to Him, the way someone keeps counting their treasure. Each person is counted by name.', source: 'Numbers 1:1 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Each in their place', text: 'Every tribe camps around the Mishkan under its own flag. Unity doesn’t mean everyone is the same. Each has its own place, all facing the same center.', source: 'Numbers 2:2' },
      { title: 'Open like the desert', text: 'Why was the Torah given in a wilderness? The Midrash says: to receive it, you have to make yourself open like the desert, owned by no one, with room for something new.', source: 'Bamidbar Rabbah 1:7' },
      { title: 'Handle holy things with care', text: 'The Kohathites carry the ark and the holiest objects on their shoulders, but only after the priests have wrapped them. Some things are too precious to handle carelessly.', source: 'Numbers 4:4–20' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 1:1', headline: 'The Torah came in a wilderness, so no one could own it.', text: 'Numbers opens with an address: “in the wilderness of Sinai.” The Midrash (Bamidbar Rabbah 1:7) asks why there. Fire, water and wilderness belong to no one, and so does Torah: “Ho, everyone who is thirsty, come to the water.” Then it turns the question on the reader: whoever does not make himself like a wilderness, <i>hefker</i>, open to all, cannot acquire wisdom. A wilderness has no walls, no deeds, no owner. A mind already full of fences and claims has no room left for anything to be given to it.', try: 'Pick one opinion you hold tightly, about politics, a person or yourself. Today read or listen to the strongest case against it, all the way through, without arguing back.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 1:49', headline: 'The Levites were kept off a census that marked who would die.', text: 'Twelve tribes are counted, every male from twenty up. Then: “Only the tribe of Levi you shall not count.” Rashi gives two reasons. The king’s own legion is counted alone. And a darker one: God foresaw that everyone counted here, twenty and older, would be sentenced to die in the wilderness (14:29). The Levites, who had refused to worship the golden calf, were kept off that list. The census that honored a generation was already the roll of those who would not reach the land. Months earlier the Levites stood apart from the crowd; now standing apart saves them.', try: 'Name one thing you do mostly because everyone around you does it. Decide today whether you would still choose it if you were the only one, and act on the answer.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 2:2', headline: 'Israel camped in the formation they once used to carry Jacob’s coffin.', text: 'God tells Moses to arrange the tribes by banners around the Tabernacle. The Midrash (Bamidbar Rabbah 2:8) says Moses was troubled: if Judah wants the south and Reuben the east, there will be fights. God answers: they don’t need you; they already have an order from their father. Before he died, Jacob told his sons exactly where each would stand around his bier, three on every side (Rashi on Genesis 50:13). The camp of Israel is a funeral procession turned around: the same places, now surrounding not a dead father but the living Presence.', try: 'If a turf fight starts today, at work or in the family, don’t invent a new rule. Look for an older agreement everyone already honors, and build on that.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 3:1', headline: 'Moses’ own sons vanish from his family tree; his students replace them.', text: '“These are the generations of Aaron and Moses,” and then only Aaron’s sons are listed. Rashi explains: they count as Moses’ children because he taught them, for whoever teaches another’s child Torah is as if he gave birth to him (Sanhedrin 19b). Moses’ own sons, Gershom and Eliezer, do not appear. The Talmud (Bava Batra 109b) even says the idol-priest Jonathan in the book of Judges was Moses’ grandson, called “son of Manasseh” because he acted like that wicked king. The greatest teacher could not pass it on at home. His legacy went to those he sat down and taught.', try: 'Choose one person younger than you, a nephew, a colleague, a friend’s kid, and today teach them something you know well, properly, sitting down and taking the time.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 3:29', headline: 'Rashi blames part of Korach’s rebellion on who camped next door.', text: 'Kehat’s families pitch their tents on the south side of the Tabernacle, and the tribe of Reuben camps in the south too. Rashi sees a warning: “Woe to the wicked, and woe to his neighbor.” Korach was a Kehatite; Datan and Aviram were Reubenites, and living beside him they were drawn into his quarrel and fell with him. On the east, beside Moses and Aaron, camped Judah, Issachar and Zebulun, and Rashi says they became the great scholars: “Good for the righteous, good for his neighbor.” No one chooses where to pitch a tent without choosing who they will become.', try: 'Think of the five people you spend the most time with, in person or online. Ask honestly which way each one pulls you, and spend time today with one who pulls you up.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 3:50', headline: 'When only some had to pay, Moses let a lottery decide.', text: 'The Levites replace the firstborn one for one, but there are 22,273 firstborn and only 22,000 Levites. The 273 left over must each pay five shekels. Rashi, from the Talmud (Sanhedrin 17a), imagines Moses’ problem: every firstborn will say, “A Levite already redeemed me.” So he writes 22,000 slips reading “a Levite” and 273 reading “five shekels,” mixes them in an urn, and has everyone draw. Moses doesn’t pretend the outcome can be equal; some will pay and some won’t. He makes the process equal, so no one can say he was singled out.', try: 'If you have to hand out something unequal today, a chore, a shift, a bill, decide how you’ll choose before you know who gets what, and tell everyone the method.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 4:18', headline: 'The family that carried the Ark was quietly dying out.', text: 'God warns Moses and Aaron: “Do not let the tribe of the Kehatite families be cut off.” The Midrash (Bamidbar Rabbah 5:1) reads the census numbers closely. Kehat is the largest Levite family, 8,600 counted from a month old, yet only 2,750 are of working age, less than a third. Its explanation: sparks from the Ark burned its bearers, so men grabbed the table or the lamp and fled the Ark, and that contempt brought more anger. God’s remedy is structure: Aaron’s sons must assign each man his task, so no one is left to grab or to flee.', try: 'Is there a job at work or at home that everyone dodges because it burns whoever holds it? Today take your fair turn at it, or set up a rotation so it stops falling on one person.' },
    ],
  },

  'Nasso': {
    meaning: 'Lift up',
    summary: 'The count of the Levite families continues, and each is given its duties. The portion includes laws about confessing and repaying wrongs, the <i>sotah</i> (a wife suspected of unfaithfulness), and the <i>nazir</i>, who vows to give up wine and haircuts for a time. Then come the words of the Priestly Blessing. Finally, each tribe’s leader brings an identical gift to dedicate the altar, one per day for twelve days.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'May God bless you', text: '“May the LORD bless you and protect you. May the LORD shine His face on you and be gracious to you. May the LORD lift His face toward you and give you peace.” It’s still said in synagogue, and by parents over their children.', source: 'Numbers 6:24–26' },
      { title: 'Same gift, different hearts', text: 'Each tribal leader brings exactly the same gift, yet the Torah lists each one in full, twelve times. The Midrash says each gift meant something different to the one who gave it.', source: 'Numbers 7 · Bamidbar Rabbah 13–14' },
      { title: 'Peace is worth everything', text: 'In the sotah ritual, God’s own name is written and dissolved in water to bring peace between a husband and wife. The Talmud: great is peace, that God lets His holy name be erased for it.', source: 'Numbers 5:23 · Chullin 141a' },
      { title: 'Confess and repay', text: 'When you wrong someone, the Torah says to confess and repay in full, plus a fifth more. Saying sorry isn’t enough. Make it right, and then some.', source: 'Numbers 5:6–7' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 4:22', headline: 'Gershon was the firstborn, and the Torah still counted him second.', text: 'The census of the Levites began with Kehat, the family that carried the Ark. Now: “Take a census of the sons of Gershon too.” But Gershon was Levi’s firstborn. Why does he come second? The Midrash (Bamidbar Rabbah 6:1) answers with a verse about Torah, “more precious than pearls,” and a ruling of the Mishnah: a <i>mamzer</i> who is a scholar takes precedence over a High Priest who is ignorant (Horayot 3:8). Kehat carried the Ark, which held the Torah, so Kehat goes first. In this camp, what you carry outranks the order you were born in.', try: 'Think of a place where you’ve been coasting on something you were handed: a title, a family name, seniority. Today do one thing there that would earn your place if you had none of it.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 4:47', headline: 'In the Temple, a Levite retired only when his voice gave out.', text: 'The Levites work from thirty to fifty at “the service of service and the service of carrying.” Rashi, from the Talmud (Arakhin 11a), says “the service of service” is music: the Levites’ song with cymbals and harps, a service that accompanies the offerings. The Talmud (Chullin 24a) adds that the age limit applied only in the desert, where the work was hauling. In Shiloh and in the Temple, a Levite was disqualified only when his voice changed and he could no longer sing. Once no one had to carry the Tabernacle, what remained of the job was the song.', try: 'Find the part of your work that is “service of service,” the thing that makes the rest worth doing, and give it real time today instead of squeezing it in at the end.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 5:7', headline: 'The Torah’s law of confession begins with cheating another person.', text: '“When a man or woman commits any of the sins of people, betraying the LORD… they shall confess the sin they committed.” Maimonides opens his Laws of Repentance with this verse: confession must be spoken aloud, and paying back the damage doesn’t atone until he confesses. Rashi says the case is someone who robbed and swore falsely. The next verse asks what happens if the victim has no relative left to repay. Rashi: a convert who died without heirs. The money still goes back, to the priest. The debt outlives the person you owed.', try: 'Is there someone you wronged and quietly made it up to without ever saying so? Today say the words to them: what you did, that it was wrong, and that you’re sorry.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 6:11', headline: 'One sage says the nazir sinned by abstaining; Ramban, by stopping.', text: 'When a nazir’s vow is interrupted, the priest atones for him “for having sinned against the soul.” What sin? Rabbi Elazar HaKappar says: he denied himself wine, and if that needs atonement, how much more someone who fasts from everything (Taanit 11a). Rabbi Elazar disagrees: the nazir is called “holy.” Ramban, on the sin offering even a faultless nazir brings at the end, says the opposite: his sin is going back to the world’s desires when he could have stayed holy. One worries about too much self-denial, the other about too little. The Torah keeps both worries alive.', try: 'Look at one thing you’ve given up and one thing you’ve let back in. Ask honestly which way you’re erring right now, and make one small correction today.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 7:9', headline: 'David called the Torah a song, and forgot how to carry it.', text: 'Moses gives the princes’ wagons to Gershon and Merari, “but to the sons of Kehat he gave none,” because the holiest things must be carried on the shoulder. Centuries later King David brings the Ark to Jerusalem on a new cart; the oxen stumble, Uzzah reaches out to steady it, and he dies. Why did he err? Rava (Sotah 35a) says: he had called the Torah “songs” in a psalm. God said: you call it songs? You will stumble on something schoolchildren know. Torah can be music, but some of it is weight, and goes on the shoulder.', try: 'Pick something you love but treat lightly, a practice, a relationship, a skill. Today do the heavy, unglamorous part of it that you’ve been skipping.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 7:48', headline: 'Ephraim’s prince brought his gift on Shabbat, when no one may.', text: 'Twelve princes bring twelve identical gifts, listed item by item twelve times, making this its longest chapter. But identical is not the same. The seventh day of the dedication falls on Shabbat, when a private offering is never brought, and it is Ephraim’s turn. The Midrash (Bamidbar Rabbah 14:2) explains: Joseph kept Shabbat in Egypt before it was ever given, telling his steward to “slaughter and prepare” on a Friday. So God said: his descendant will offer on Shabbat, and I will gladly accept it. The same gift, coming out of a different history, means something else.', try: 'Do one routine kindness today, the call, the favor, the coffee, and add one detail that could only have come from you.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 7:89', headline: 'God was speaking to Himself, and Moses learned to overhear.', text: 'After twelve days of gifts, the portion ends quietly: Moses enters the Tent “and he heard the Voice speaking to him.” Rashi notes the odd verb, <i>midaber</i>, which he reads as <i>mitdaber</i>: the Voice was speaking to itself, and Moses listened in. Rashi adds that it was the same thunderous Voice heard at Sinai, yet it stopped at the Tent’s door and went no farther. The Voice wasn’t lowered or shrunk to fit Moses. He had to come in to where it was already sounding. Revelation here is less a broadcast than a conversation already underway.', try: 'Before your next conversation today, sit ten minutes with a psalm or a page of Torah and listen for what it is saying, not for what you want from it.' },
    ],
  },

  'Beha\'alotcha': {
    meaning: 'When you raise up',
    summary: 'Aaron lights the menorah, and the Levites begin their service. The Israelites celebrate Passover in the desert, with a second chance for those who missed it. The cloud leads them and silver trumpets sound as they set out from Sinai. Then the people complain about the manna and crave meat, and Moses, overwhelmed, is given seventy elders to share the load. Miriam and Aaron speak against Moses; Miriam is struck with tzara’at, and the whole people wait for her for seven days.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Help others shine on their own', text: 'Aaron is told to light the lamps “when you raise them up.” Rashi says: hold the flame to the wick until it rises by itself. Good teachers and parents help others shine on their own.', source: 'Numbers 8:2 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Follow the cloud', text: 'Sometimes the cloud over the Mishkan stayed for a day, sometimes for a year, and the people moved only when it moved. Learning to wait, and to go, on a timing that isn’t yours is hard and important.', source: 'Numbers 9:17–23' },
      { title: 'Even Moses gets overwhelmed', text: '“I can’t carry this whole people by myself; it’s too heavy for me.” Moses admits it, and God gives him seventy elders to help. Asking for help is wisdom, not weakness.', source: 'Numbers 11:14–17' },
      { title: 'The humblest man', text: 'When Miriam and Aaron criticize him, Moses doesn’t defend himself; he was “very humble, more than anyone on earth.” Then he prays for Miriam in five Hebrew words: “Please, God, heal her, please.”', source: 'Numbers 12:3, 13' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 8:2', headline: 'Aaron, left out of the dedication, was promised Hanukkah.', text: 'For twelve days the tribal princes have brought gifts, and Aaron’s tribe had none. Rashi, from the Midrash, says Aaron was disheartened, and God consoled him: “Yours is greater than theirs, for you will kindle the lamps.” Ramban is puzzled. Why the lamps, and not the incense or the offerings only priests bring? The Temple lamps went dark with the Temple too. His answer: the Midrash is hinting at Hanukkah, the lights Aaron’s descendants, the Hasmoneans, would kindle, which Jews still light in exile. The one who felt left out was given the only part that would outlast the building.', try: 'Think of something you were left out of that mattered to you. Today find the smaller, steadier part of it that is still yours, and do it as if it will last the longest.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 8:24', headline: 'Rashi: a student who sees no progress in five years never will.', text: 'Here Levites start at twenty-five; in chapter 4, at thirty. Rashi reconciles them: at twenty-five a Levite came to learn the laws of his service, studied for five years, and only at thirty began to serve. Then he draws a hard rule from it, out of the Talmud (Chullin 24a): a student who sees no good sign in his learning within five years will never see it. The Talmud argues only about the number: Rabbi Yose says three, depending how hard the subject is. Nobody disputes that, after long enough, a path is telling you something.', try: 'Name one thing you’ve worked at for years without a real sign of progress. Ask what a good sign would even look like, and if you’ve never seen one, change the method or the path.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 9:7', headline: 'The men carrying Joseph’s coffin demanded a second chance, and got one.', text: 'Some men are impure from a dead body and can’t bring the Passover offering. Who were they? Rabbi Yose HaGelili says the bearers of Joseph’s coffin (Sukkah 25a); others say the cousins who buried Nadav and Avihu. Their impurity came from doing a mitzvah. They don’t accept the rule quietly: “Why should we be held back?” Moses says, “Wait, and I will hear.” The answer is <i>Pesach Sheni</i>, a second Passover a month later. Rashi says this law should have come through Moses like all the others, but it came through them: good things come through those who deserve them.', try: 'Is there something you were shut out of by a rule that wasn’t your fault? Today ask the person in charge, plainly, “Why should I be left out?” and see whether there is a second Passover.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 10:9', headline: 'Maimonides calls it cruel to shrug off a disaster as bad luck.', text: '“When you go to war against an enemy who oppresses you, sound the trumpets.” From this verse Maimonides makes a commandment: for every trouble that strikes the community, cry out and sound the trumpets. It is one of the paths of repentance: the sound makes everyone ask what in their own conduct this might be about. And if people instead say, “It’s just the way of the world, a chance event,” he calls that cruelty, <i>achzariut</i>, because it lets them stay attached to their deeds. Bad luck asks nothing of you. That is what makes it so comfortable.', try: 'Take one thing that went wrong this week that you’ve been calling bad luck. Ask honestly what part of it, even a small part, was yours, and change that part.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 10:33', headline: 'Israel left Sinai like a child running out of school.', text: 'After almost a year at Sinai, the cloud lifts: “They set out from the mountain of the LORD, a three days’ journey.” It sounds triumphant. But Ramban quotes an old Midrash that hears it differently: they left Sinai happily, “like a child who runs away from school,” thinking, if we stay He may give us more commandments. Ramban counts this as the first of the wilderness failures. Nobody rebels; they are only relieved to get away. The trouble begins not with a sin but with a sigh of relief at leaving the place that asked the most of them.', try: 'Notice what you are most relieved to get away from this week: a class, a commitment, a person who asks a lot of you. Go back to it today for one more hour.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 11:5', headline: 'They missed Egypt’s free fish: free of commandments, Rashi says.', text: 'The people weep for meat: “We remember the fish we ate in Egypt for free, the cucumbers, the melons, the leeks.” Rashi doesn’t believe it. In Egypt they weren’t even given straw for free; would anyone have handed slaves free fish? So what does <i>chinam</i>, “for free,” mean? Free of commandments. They weren’t missing the food. They were missing a life in which nothing was asked of them, and slavery, remembered from far enough away, looked like that. Nostalgia edits the past: it cuts out the whip and keeps the menu.', try: 'Think of a past chapter you miss. Write down honestly what it cost you, then ask what in your life now you are trying to escape by missing it.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 12:13', headline: 'Moses’ shortest prayer was for the sister who had spoken against him.', text: 'Miriam has criticized Moses behind his back, and now she is struck white with <i>tzara’at</i>. The Torah has just called Moses the humblest man on earth. He doesn’t say she deserved it. He cries out five Hebrew words: <i>El na refa na lah</i>, “God, please, heal her, please.” Why so short? Rashi, from the Sifrei: so no one could say his sister is suffering while he stands making long speeches; or, in another version, that he prays at length for family but not for us. There is no grievance in the prayer at all, only the request.', try: 'Think of someone who said something unfair about you. Today wish them well, in a prayer or out loud, in one short sentence, without adding anything about yourself.' },
    ],
  },

  'Sh\'lach': {
    meaning: 'Send',
    summary: 'Moses sends twelve scouts, one from each tribe, to explore the land of Canaan. They come back carrying huge fruit, but ten say the people there are giants and the land can’t be taken; only Caleb and Joshua say, “We can do it.” The people panic and want to go back to Egypt, so God decrees forty years of wandering, until a new generation grows up. The portion ends with laws including tzitzit, the fringes on the corners of clothes.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'How you see yourself', text: 'The scouts say, “We looked like grasshoppers in our own eyes, and so we must have looked to them.” How we see ourselves shapes how others see us.', source: 'Numbers 13:33' },
      { title: 'Same facts, different story', text: 'All twelve saw the same land. Ten saw giants; two saw a land “flowing with milk and honey.” What you look for decides what you find.', source: 'Numbers 13:27–30 · 14:6–9' },
      { title: 'A different spirit', text: 'God praises Caleb: “He had a different spirit in him, and followed Me completely.” One person who refuses to go along with the crowd can change history.', source: 'Numbers 14:24' },
      { title: 'Fear spreads fast', text: 'Ten frightened men talk a whole nation into panic overnight. Bad news and fear travel faster than hope. Be careful what you pass along.', source: 'Numbers 13:32–14:1' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 13:2', headline: 'God allowed the spies, knowing they would lead the people astray.', text: '“Send for yourself men to scout the land.” Rashi, following Reish Lakish in the Talmud (Sotah 34b), hears the “for yourself”: by your own judgment; I am not commanding you. The idea came from the people (Deuteronomy 1:22). Then Rashi, from the Midrash, gives God’s answer: I already told them the land is good. By their lives, I will give them room to go wrong through the spies’ words, so that they will not inherit it. God doesn’t stop a mistake He can see coming. The freedom to demand proof includes the freedom to be ruined by it.', try: 'Is there a question you keep asking about something you already know is good, a person, a commitment, a plan? Today notice whether you want an answer, or an exit.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 13:33', headline: '“We were grasshoppers in our own eyes,” and only then in theirs.', text: 'The scouts come back with fruit and fear: giants live there, “and we were like grasshoppers in our own eyes, and so were we in their eyes.” Rav Mesharshiya in the Talmud (Sotah 35a) calls them liars. In their own eyes, fine. But how could they know how they looked to the giants? The Talmud does offer a defense: they sat in the cedars above a Canaanite mourners’ meal and overheard them say, “There are people like grasshoppers up there.” Either way, notice the order of the sentence. They felt small first. What others saw came second.', try: 'Before a conversation today where you feel small, write down how you think the other person sees you. Then ask what evidence you have for it besides your own eyes.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 14:24', headline: 'Caleb survived the spies by hiding what he really thought.', text: 'God spares Caleb “because a different spirit was with him.” Rashi reads “different” as double: one spirit in his mouth and another in his heart. To the other scouts he said, “I am with you,” while he meant all along to tell the truth. The Talmud (Sotah 35a) shows how it worked. With the crowd roaring, Caleb shouted, “Is this all the son of Amram has done to us?” Everyone fell silent, expecting an attack on Moses, and into that silence he said: the land is good, we can do it. Integrity sometimes needs timing, and a little cunning.', try: 'If you need to say something unpopular today, don’t blurt it into the noise. Plan the moment when people will actually hear it, and then say all of it.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 14:27', headline: 'Every minyan is counted from the ten spies.', text: '“How long shall I bear this evil congregation?” God means the ten scouts who brought back the bad report. Rashi, from the Talmud (Megillah 23b): this is where we learn that a “congregation,” an <i>edah</i>, is ten. So the quorum for the holiest moments of prayer, <i>Kaddish</i>, <i>Kedushah</i>, the reading of the Torah, is defined by the ten men who talked a whole nation out of its future. The rabbis could have chosen a kinder source. They chose this one, which says plainly what ten people of one mind can do, for ruin or for holiness.', try: 'Show up today to make up a minyan, or any group that needs one more person to happen. And in a group where the talk turns sour, be the voice that turns it back.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 15:15', headline: 'Every convert enters the covenant the way Israel did at Sinai.', text: '“As you are, so shall the stranger be before the LORD.” The verse is about offerings: a convert who brings one follows the same rules as anyone born Jewish. Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi reads it more deeply: “as you are” means as your ancestors were (Keritot 9a). Just as Israel entered the covenant through circumcision, immersion and the sprinkling of an offering’s blood, so does every convert. A convert is not a later, lesser copy of the Jewish people. Each one walks through the same door the whole people walked through at the mountain.', try: 'If you know someone who converted, or is on the way, ask them today what drew them, and listen the way you would to an eyewitness of Sinai.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 15:26', headline: 'Yom Kippur begins by declaring that everyone sinned by mistake.', text: 'This section deals with a whole community that goes astray without knowing it. It ends: “All the congregation of Israel shall be forgiven, and the stranger living among them, for all the people acted in error.” This is the verse sung right after Kol Nidre, three times, as Yom Kippur begins. It is a generous fiction, and the Talmud supplies its logic. Reish Lakish says repentance is so great that deliberate sins become like mistakes, and when it comes from love, even like merits (Yoma 86b). The holiest day opens by treating everyone in the room as someone who stumbled.', try: 'Choose one person whose wrong you’ve filed as deliberate. Today try on the Kol Nidre reading, that they stumbled, and see whether it changes how you speak to them.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 15:39', headline: 'The portion that began with spies ends by calling your eyes spies.', text: 'The portion opened with men sent <i>latur</i>, to scout the land. It ends with the fringes: look at them and remember, <i>v’lo taturu</i>, “and do not scout after your heart and your eyes.” Rashi makes the link: the heart and the eyes are the body’s scouts. The eye sees, the heart desires, the body does the deed. Ten men saw giants and their hearts melted; each of us runs a small version of that every day. Rashi also counts the fringes: <i>tzitzit</i> is 600, plus eight threads and five knots, 613, every commandment hanging from a corner.', try: 'Today notice the first thing your eyes reach for when you’re bored, and before your heart follows, pause and ask whether you want what they found.' },
    ],
  },

  'Korach': {
    who: 'Korach, a cousin of Moses',
    summary: 'Korach, a Levite, leads a rebellion with Datan, Aviram and 250 leaders, challenging Moses and Aaron: “The whole community is holy. Why do you raise yourselves above everyone?” The earth swallows the rebels and fire consumes the 250. When the people blame Moses, a plague breaks out until Aaron stands between the living and the dead with incense. Aaron’s staff blossoms with almonds to show God’s choice, and the duties and gifts of the priests and Levites are spelled out.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Arguments for the right reasons', text: 'The Mishnah says an argument “for the sake of Heaven,” like Hillel and Shammai’s, will last; one that isn’t, like Korach’s, won’t. Ask yourself whether you’re arguing to find the truth or to win.', source: 'Pirkei Avot 5:17' },
      { title: 'Envy dressed as fairness', text: 'Korach says “everyone is holy,” but he wants the power for himself. Rashi says jealousy over a cousin’s promotion set him off. Watch out for complaints that are really envy.', source: 'Numbers 16:1–3 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Love peace and chase it', text: 'Hillel taught: “Be like the students of Aaron, loving peace and pursuing peace.” In the middle of a rebellion against him, Aaron’s answer is to run into the crowd and save lives.', source: 'Pirkei Avot 1:12 · Numbers 17:11–13' },
      { title: 'God is their inheritance', text: 'Aaron’s family gets no land: “I am your portion and your inheritance.” Some people’s wealth is in what they serve, not what they own.', source: 'Numbers 18:20' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 16:7', headline: 'Korach foresaw his own great descendants, and it destroyed him.', text: 'Korach was a clever man, Rashi says. What drove him to this? His eye misled him. He saw in a vision that the prophet Samuel, whom a psalm ranks with Moses and Aaron, would descend from him, and choirs of Levites singing by the holy spirit. Can all this come from me, he thought, and I stay silent? When Moses said only the one God chooses would survive, he assumed it meant him. He saw truly but read wrongly: the greatness would come through his sons, who repented and lived. A true glimpse of your future can still ruin you.', try: 'Think of a big future you feel sure you’re meant for. Today ask what it requires of you now, patience, humility, letting someone else go first, rather than what it entitles you to.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 16:19', headline: 'Korach spent the night telling each tribe he fought for them.', text: 'The showdown is set for morning, and Korach doesn’t sleep. Rashi says he spent the whole night going from tribe to tribe: “Do you think I’m doing this for myself? I’m doing it for all of you. These men grab every high office: the throne for himself, the priesthood for his brother.” By morning, “Korach gathered the whole congregation against them.” Notice what he sells. Not a plan, not a vision, only a grievance shared out evenly. And it is false: Moses gave his own sons nothing. Flattery that names an enemy is the cheapest way to build a following.', try: 'The next time someone tells you they’re only fighting for you, ask what they get if they win, and whether they have any plan beyond the enemy.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 17:5', headline: 'The rebels’ fire-pans were hammered into the altar as a warning.', text: 'After the fire, Elazar collects the copper fire-pans of the two hundred fifty men and hammers them into plating for the altar, “a reminder… so that no outsider come near… and not be like Korach and his company.” Rav in the Talmud (Sanhedrin 110a) turns that last phrase into a commandment for all time: whoever keeps a dispute going violates it. Reish Lakish notes that Moses himself went to Datan and Aviram, though they were in the wrong. Every priest who climbed the altar touched the metal of a rebellion. Some arguments must be ended and remembered, not continued.', try: 'Pick one quarrel you’ve kept alive, even only in your head. Today take one step to end it, a message, an apology, or a real decision to drop it, even if you were the one wronged.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 17:13', headline: 'The incense they called poison was what stopped the plague.', text: 'The day after Korach falls, the people turn on Moses: “You have killed the LORD’s people!” A plague breaks out. Moses tells Aaron to take incense and run into the crowd, and Aaron stands “between the dead and the living.” Why incense? Rashi explains: the people were calling incense a deadly poison. Nadav and Avihu died offering it, and so did the two hundred fifty. God said: you will see it can stop a plague; it is sin that kills. The thing they feared was never the danger. What people did with it was.', try: 'Think of something you’ve written off because it once went badly, a practice, a kind of conversation, a place. Ask whether the problem was the thing or how it was used, and try it again properly.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 17:21', headline: 'Moses put Aaron’s staff in the middle, so nobody could cry foul.', text: 'To end the argument, each tribal leader’s name goes on a staff, and all of them, Aaron’s too, lie in the Tent overnight. Rashi notices the words “Aaron’s staff was among their staffs.” Moses placed it in the middle, so no one could say it blossomed only because it lay on the side nearest the Presence. Moses knows whom God chose, but sets up the test as if he didn’t, so the losers can trust the result. By morning Aaron’s dry stick has blossomed and ripened almonds. Being right is not enough. People need a fair way to see it.', try: 'If you’re in a disagreement you’re sure you’re right about, propose a way to settle it that the other side would call fair, even one that gives them a real chance to win.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 18:20', headline: 'Maimonides says anyone can join the tribe that owns nothing.', text: 'God tells Aaron: “You shall have no inheritance in their land… I am your portion and your inheritance.” Priests and Levites get no fields; they live on gifts and serve. Maimonides ends his Laws of the Sabbatical Year with a stunning extension. Not only the tribe of Levi, but any person in the world whose spirit moves him to stand before God, to serve Him and to know Him, and who throws off the yoke of the many calculations people chase, becomes holy of holies. God will be his portion and provide what he needs, as for the priests.', try: 'Name one calculation that runs your days: money, status, what people will think. Today make one real decision as if God, not that calculation, were your portion.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 18:26', headline: 'The Levites lived on gifts, and still had to give a tenth.', text: 'Israel gives the Levites a tenth of its harvest. Then God tells Moses to tell the Levites: from that tenth, you give a tenth too, to the priests. Rashi, on the next verse, says this tithe of a tithe is as holy as the farmer’s great offering, “like grain from the threshing floor,” with the same strict laws. Elsewhere the Talmud makes it a general rule (Gittin 7b): even a poor person who lives on charity must give charity. No one is reduced to being only a receiver. Giving is part of everyone’s dignity.', try: 'If you’re in a season of receiving, help, money, favors, give something anyway today, a small sum or an hour, to someone who needs it more than you.' },
    ],
  },

  'Chukat': {
    meaning: 'The law of',
    summary: 'The mysterious law of the red heifer, whose ashes purify people who have been near death. Miriam dies, and the people have no water. Told to speak to a rock, Moses strikes it in anger, and God says he won’t lead the people into the land. Aaron dies on Mount Hor. Snakes attack the camp, and a copper snake on a pole heals those who look at it. Israel defeats the kings Sichon and Og and camps on the plains of Moab.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Not everything makes sense', text: 'The red heifer’s ashes purify the impure, but make the priest who prepares them impure. The Midrash says even wise King Solomon couldn’t understand it. Some things we do out of trust, not understanding.', source: 'Numbers 19 · Bamidbar Rabbah 19:3' },
      { title: 'More is asked of the great', text: 'One angry moment at the rock keeps Moses out of the promised land. The Talmud says God is exacting with those closest to Him “to a hair’s breadth.” The greater the person, the higher the standard.', source: 'Numbers 20:12 · Yevamot 121b' },
      { title: 'When Miriam dies, the water stops', text: 'Right after Miriam dies, “there was no water for the community.” The Talmud says a well traveled with the people in her merit. Some people sustain us so quietly that we notice only when they’re gone.', source: 'Numbers 20:1–2 · Taanit 9a' },
      { title: 'Look up and heal', text: 'Bitten by snakes, the people look at a copper snake on a pole and are healed. The Mishnah says it wasn’t the snake that healed them. It was looking up, and turning their hearts to God.', source: 'Numbers 21:8–9 · Mishnah Rosh Hashanah 3:8' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 19:2', headline: 'Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai admitted the water doesn’t actually purify.', text: 'The red heifer is the Torah’s great riddle: its ashes in water purify someone who touched the dead. The Midrash (Bamidbar Rabbah 19:8) tells of a gentile who called it sorcery. Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai answered with something familiar, an exorcism: sprinkle water and the impure spirit flees. When the man left, his students said: him you pushed off with a reed; what will you tell us? He said: by your lives, the dead do not defile and the water does not purify. God made a decree. The outsider got a reed to lean on. His students got the truth.', try: 'Pick one practice you keep but can’t fully explain. Instead of inventing a reason for it today, keep it honestly as a commitment, and say so if anyone asks.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 20:2', headline: 'Only when Miriam died did they learn the water had been hers.', text: '“Miriam died there and was buried there.” No mourning is described, no eulogy. The very next verse: “And there was no water for the congregation.” Rashi, from the Talmud (Taanit 9a), draws the conclusion: for forty years they had the well in Miriam’s merit. The Talmud names three providers, Moses, Aaron and Miriam, and three gifts, the manna, the cloud and the well. The people never thanked her for water they didn’t know was hers. They found out by being thirsty. Some people hold up a whole community, and the proof arrives the day they’re gone.', try: 'Think of someone whose quiet work keeps your life running, a parent, a partner, a colleague. Tell them today, specifically, what would dry up without them.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 20:10', headline: 'No one agrees what Moses did wrong at the rock.', text: 'For striking the rock, Moses is barred from the land he spent forty years leading toward. But what was the sin? Rashi: he hit the rock instead of speaking to it. Maimonides: he lost his temper, “Listen, you rebels,” and the people took his anger for God’s. Ramban quotes that and dismisses it as “vanity upon vanities,” preferring Rabbeinu Chananel: Moses said “shall <i>we</i> bring you water,” as if the miracle were theirs. Then Ramban adds that the true answer is one of the Torah’s deep secrets. The harshest sentence on its greatest man, and the charge is left unclear.', try: 'Take one failure of yours you think you understand. Today write down three different explanations of what went wrong, not only the one you usually tell.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 20:17', headline: 'Israel had a miraculous well, and still offered to buy Edom’s water.', text: 'Moses asks the king of Edom for passage: we won’t cross your fields, “and we will not drink well water.” Rashi notices it should say cistern water, and explains: we have manna to eat and a well that travels with us, but we won’t use them here. We’ll buy food and water from you, so you profit. From this, Rashi says, a guest who has his own food should still buy from the local shopkeeper, to benefit his host. Edom refuses and comes out armed. The offer was still the right one: pass through someone’s land and leave them richer.', try: 'Wherever you are a guest or a visitor today, a friend’s home, a new neighborhood, spend something there that leaves them better off for your visit.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 21:8', headline: 'The bronze snake that healed Israel later had to be smashed.', text: 'Bitten by snakes, the people beg Moses to pray. God has him make a snake and set it on a pole: whoever is bitten and looks at it will live. The Mishnah (Rosh Hashanah 3:8) asks: does a snake kill, or give life? Rather, when Israel looked upward and bent their hearts to their Father in heaven, they were healed. The snake only gave their eyes a direction. Centuries later King Hezekiah smashed it, because people were burning incense to it (II Kings 18:4). Anything that points you to God can, kept long enough, become what you worship.', try: 'Look at one thing you rely on to feel close to something larger, a ritual, a teacher, a place. Today ask whether it still points beyond itself, or has become the point.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 21:14', headline: 'In a forgotten place-name, the Talmud hears love after the fight.', text: 'The Torah quotes a vanished book, “the Book of the Wars of the LORD,” with a line no one can quite translate: <i>Vahev b’Sufah</i>, apparently a place near the Arnon. The Talmud (Kiddushin 30b) hears it differently. Rabbi Chiya bar Abba says even a father and son, or a teacher and student, who study Torah together become enemies in the heat of argument, and they don’t leave until they love each other: <i>vahev</i>, love, <i>b’sofah</i>, at its end. The real wars of the Lord are the fights over what His words mean. Fought right, they end in love.', try: 'Have a real argument today with someone you respect, about an idea, not a person. Don’t walk away until you can tell them what you’ve come to admire in how they think.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 21:34', headline: 'Moses feared Og because of one favor Og did Abraham.', text: 'Moses defeats Sihon without hesitation, but before Og, God has to say: “Do not fear him.” Why? Rashi says Moses was afraid Abraham’s merit would protect Og, since Og was “the one who escaped” and came to tell Abraham that Lot had been captured. Centuries later, that one act still counts. And Rashi on Genesis, from the Midrash, says Og’s motive was rotten: he hoped Abraham would die in battle so he could marry Sarah. Moses still feared the merit of a good deed done for a bad reason. Even a self-serving kindness leaves something real in the world.', try: 'Think of someone you dislike who once did you a real favor, whatever their motive. Today acknowledge it, to them or at least out loud to yourself.' },
    ],
  },

  'Balak': {
    who: 'Balak, king of Moab',
    summary: 'Balak, king of Moab, terrified of Israel, hires the prophet Bilaam to curse them. On the way, Bilaam’s donkey sees an angel blocking the road and refuses to go on, and then speaks. Three times Bilaam tries to curse Israel from a mountaintop, and three times only blessings come out, including “How good are your tents, O Jacob.” The portion ends with Israelites sinning with Moabite women, and Pinchas stopping a plague.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Even a donkey can see', text: 'Bilaam, the famous seer, can’t see the angel in his path, but his donkey can. Sometimes the ones we look down on see what we’re too proud to notice.', source: 'Numbers 22:22–33' },
      { title: 'Curses turned into blessings', text: 'Every time Bilaam opens his mouth to curse Israel, a blessing comes out. Moses later reminds the people: “God turned the curse into a blessing for you, because He loves you.”', source: 'Deuteronomy 23:6' },
      { title: 'Doors that don’t face each other', text: 'Rashi says Bilaam saw that Israel’s tents were set up so no doorway looked into another’s home, and that’s what moved him to bless them. Respecting other people’s privacy is a kind of holiness.', source: 'Numbers 24:2 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'A people that stands apart', text: 'Bilaam calls Israel “a people that dwells alone, not counted among the nations.” Being different can be a strength.', source: 'Numbers 23:9' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 22:12', headline: 'God told Bilaam: they need neither your honey nor your sting.', text: 'Balak’s messengers arrive, and God tells Bilaam: “Do not go with them. Do not curse the people, for they are blessed.” Rashi fills in the bargaining behind each phrase. Then I’ll curse them from here, Bilaam says. Do not curse. Then I’ll bless them. They don’t need your blessing; they are already blessed. And Rashi adds what people say to a hornet: neither your honey nor your sting. Bilaam can’t imagine a people who don’t need him either way. Some people’s praise is only another way of holding power over you.', try: 'Think of someone whose approval you still chase though they’ve hurt you. Today do one thing you value without telling them, and notice that it stands without their honey.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 22:18', headline: 'Bilaam’s pious refusal named a bigger price than Balak offered.', text: 'Balak’s grander second delegation promises honor. Bilaam answers piously: “Even if Balak gave me his house full of silver and gold, I could not go against the word of the LORD.” Bilaam is the one who brings up silver and gold, and Rashi hears the greed in it: Balak ought to give me all of it, he reasoned, since armies might lose and I would surely win. The Mishnah (Avot 5:19) makes him the model of three traits: an evil eye, a haughty spirit and a greedy soul. His refusal already contained the bid.', try: 'Listen to your own refusals today. When you say “I would never…,” notice what you named, and ask whether part of you is negotiating.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 22:21', headline: 'Bilaam saddled his own donkey, as Abraham did going to Moriah.', text: '“Bilaam rose in the morning and saddled his donkey.” A man of his rank had servants for that. Rashi, from the Midrash: hatred makes a person throw off the dignity of his station; he did it himself, eagerly. And God said: wicked one, their father Abraham got there before you, for “Abraham rose early in the morning and saddled his donkey” to go to the binding of Isaac. The same early start, the same hands on the same strap. Love and hatred both get people out of bed early. The question is which one is doing it for you.', try: 'Notice what got you moving fastest this week, the thing you did eagerly, yourself, without being asked. Ask honestly whether it was love or a grudge.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 23:10', headline: 'Bilaam prayed to die like the righteous, and died by the sword.', text: 'From the mountaintop Bilaam sees the camp and wishes instead: “May I die the death of the upright, and may my end be like theirs.” Rashi reads it simply: let me die among them. He wanted the ending without the life. The Torah answers him later in one line: when Israel fought Midian, “Bilaam son of Beor they killed with the sword” (31:8), and Rashi says he unknowingly foretold it in his own oracle. A death like the righteous is not something you can ask for at the end. It is the last line of a life written all along.', try: 'Write one sentence you would want said about you at your funeral. Then do one thing today that would make it true.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 23:24', headline: 'Bilaam watched Israel wake up in the morning like lions.', text: '“Here is a people that rises like a lioness, lifts itself up like a lion.” Bilaam means a predator. Rashi hears a morning routine: when they rise from sleep they spring up like lions to grab the commandments, to wrap themselves in the tallit, read the Shema and put on tefillin. At night they don’t lie down until they’ve said the Shema. The enemy saw the camp’s strength not in its army but in how it began and ended each day. The first minutes of your morning say, to everyone including you, what you are for.', try: 'Decide today what the first ten minutes of tomorrow will hold, before the phone, and then grab them like something valuable.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 24:5', headline: 'What moved Bilaam to bless: tent doors that didn’t face each other.', text: 'Bilaam looks out and sees “Israel dwelling tribe by tribe,” and the spirit of God comes on him. What did he see? Rashi, from the Talmud (Bava Batra 60a): their tent doors were not lined up facing each other, so no one could see into a neighbor’s home. Then come the words that open our morning prayers: “How good are your tents, O Jacob.” In the Talmud (Sanhedrin 105b) those tents are synagogues and houses of study, and Rabbi Abba bar Kahana adds a sober note: every blessing Bilaam spoke turned back into the curse he meant, except that one.', try: 'Protect someone’s privacy today: don’t forward the screenshot, don’t repeat the story, don’t look where you weren’t invited.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 24:17', headline: 'Rabbi Akiva read Bilaam’s “star” as Bar Kokhba, and was wrong.', text: 'Bilaam’s last vision: “I see him, but not now… a star steps forth from Jacob.” Rabbi Akiva read the star as Shimon bar Koziba, leader of the revolt against Rome, remembered as Bar Kokhba, “son of a star.” He declared, “This is the King Messiah.” Rabbi Yochanan ben Torta answered: Akiva, grass will grow from your cheeks and the son of David will not yet have come (Jerusalem Talmud, Taanit 4:5). The revolt was crushed at Betar. Maimonides notes that Akiva and the sages of his generation believed it until Bar Koziba was killed. Bilaam had said it: not now.', try: 'Name one hope you’ve pinned on a person or a plan arriving right now. Keep the hope, but write down today what you will do if the answer is “not now.”' },
    ],
  },

  'Pinchas': {
    who: 'Pinchas, Aaron’s grandson',
    summary: 'God rewards Pinchas with a covenant of peace. A new census counts the generation that will enter the land. The five daughters of Tzelofchad ask to inherit their father’s land, and God says they’re right. Moses is told he will see the land but not enter it, and Joshua is chosen to lead after him. The portion ends with the offerings for every day, Shabbat, the new moon, and each festival.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Speak up for what’s fair', text: 'Machlah, Noa, Choglah, Milkah and Tirtzah ask for their late father’s land. Moses brings their case to God, who answers: “The daughters of Tzelofchad are right.” Their question changed the law.', source: 'Numbers 27:1–7' },
      { title: 'A covenant of peace', text: 'Pinchas acted with fierce zeal, and God gives him “My covenant of peace.” The rabbis were wary of zealots, and many read the reward as a cure: peace to calm the fire.', source: 'Numbers 25:12 · Netziv' },
      { title: 'A leader for every spirit', text: 'Moses asks God, “the God of the spirits of all flesh,” for a leader so the people won’t be “like sheep without a shepherd.” Rashi: a leader has to be able to handle each person’s different spirit.', source: 'Numbers 27:16–17 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Every day counts', text: 'The daily offering came every morning and every evening, holidays or not. One sage called “one lamb in the morning and one at dusk” the most important verse in the Torah: steady faithfulness matters most.', source: 'Numbers 28:4' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 25:12', headline: 'The Talmud says: if a zealot asks permission, don’t give it.', text: 'God rewards Pinchas, who speared Zimri and the Midianite woman, with “My covenant of peace.” The Talmud is far less comfortable. Rav Chisda rules: if someone comes to the court asking whether he may do what Pinchas did, we do not tell him yes (Sanhedrin 82a). Had Zimri pulled away first, Pinchas would have been executed for murder; had Zimri turned and killed him, Zimri would be innocent, defending himself. The Netziv reads the reward as protection: killing with your own hands leaves a mark, so God gave Pinchas peace, that he not become a man of rage.', try: 'When you feel righteous anger today, ask Rav Chisda’s question: if I asked someone wise for permission, would they give it? If not, don’t act on it.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 26:5', headline: 'In the census, God signs His own name on every family.', text: 'After the plague the people are counted again, family by family: <i>haChanochi</i>, <i>haPalu’i</i>, each name wrapped in a <i>heh</i> in front and a <i>yod</i> at the end. Rashi, from the Midrash, says the nations were sneering: how can these people trace their tribes? If the Egyptians ruled their bodies, surely they had their wives too. So God set His name, <i>Yah</i>, on every family, as if to say: I testify that they are their fathers’ sons. After a chapter of sexual scandal, the census answers with God vouching for the faithfulness of the mothers.', try: 'Think of someone whose reputation is being doubted around you. Today vouch for them out loud, to someone whose opinion matters.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 26:64', headline: 'Rashi says the wilderness decree never fell on the women.', text: 'The new census ends with a verdict: “Among these there was not a man” of those counted at Sinai; all had died in the desert, as God swore. Rashi reads the word “man” precisely. The decree after the spies fell on the men, not the women, because the women loved the land. The men had said, “Let us appoint a leader and go back to Egypt.” The women said, “Give us a holding in the land.” That is why, Rashi says, the daughters of Tzelofchad come right next. The generation that refused the land was not the whole generation.', try: 'Find the person in your circle who still wants what everyone else has given up on. Today ask them why, and listen as if they might be right.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 27:16', headline: 'Moses asked for a leader who could bear each person’s spirit.', text: 'Told he will die without entering the land, Moses doesn’t plead for himself here. He asks God for a successor, and the name he uses is unusual: “God of the spirits of all flesh.” Rashi explains: Master of the world, You know each person’s mind, and no two are alike; appoint over them someone who can bear each one according to his own spirit. After forty years of complaint and rebellion, Moses knows what a leader actually needs. Not one vision pressed onto everyone, but the patience to carry six hundred thousand different temperaments.', try: 'Pick the person you find hardest to work or live with. Today deal with them on their terms, the way their mind works, not yours.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 28:15', headline: 'Once a month, God asks for atonement for shrinking the moon.', text: 'Every new moon brings a goat as a sin offering “to the LORD,” a phrase used for no other. Reish Lakish explains it with a story (Chullin 60b). The sun and moon were created equal, and the moon asked: can two kings share one crown? God said: then go make yourself smaller. The moon protested: because I said something reasonable, I must shrink? God offered consolations: Israel will count by you; righteous men will be called “small” like you, Jacob, Samuel, David. The moon was not consoled. So God said: bring an atonement for Me, for diminishing the moon.', try: 'Think of someone you made smaller, even for a good reason: a correction, a demotion, a joke. Today acknowledge to them that it cost them something.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 29:1', headline: 'The shofar’s cry is measured by an enemy mother’s weeping.', text: 'The Torah calls Rosh Hashanah only “a day of <i>teruah</i>” and never says what a teruah sounds like. The Talmud (Rosh Hashanah 33b) finds out through the Aramaic translation, <i>yevava</i>, which points to Sisera’s mother at her window, who “wailed,” <i>vateyabev</i>, waiting for a son who would never come home. Some sages hear long groans, others short sobs, and so today we blow both. The holiest sound of the year is modeled on the grief of the mother of Israel’s enemy. Perhaps repentance begins with hearing how the other side cries.', try: 'Think of someone on the other side of a conflict of yours. Today imagine, concretely, what it has cost the people who love them.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 29:13', headline: 'Sukkot’s seventy bulls were offered for the nations of the world.', text: 'On the first day of Sukkot, thirteen bulls; then twelve, eleven, down to seven: seventy in all. Rabbi Elazar in the Talmud (Sukkah 55b) says they stand for the seventy nations of the world, brought to atone for them. Then on Shemini Atzeret, a single bull, for the single nation, Israel. Rabbi Yochanan adds a lament: woe to the nations, who lost something and don’t know what they lost; while the Temple stood the altar atoned for them, and now who does? Israel’s most joyful festival was spent, day after day, praying for everyone else.', try: 'Pray today, or simply wish well with real attention, for a people or a country you have no tie to, or a grudge against.' },
    ],
  },

  'Matot': {
    meaning: 'Tribes',
    summary: 'Moses teaches the tribal leaders about vows: a promise must be kept. Israel goes to war against Midian, and the soldiers bring back spoils that must be purified and shared. The tribes of Reuben and Gad, who have large herds, ask to settle east of the Jordan. Moses is angry until they promise to fight alongside their brothers first, and only then go home.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'War leaves a mark', text: 'Even after a war God commanded, soldiers must stay outside the camp for seven days and purify themselves. Taking life, even when it was necessary, leaves a mark that needs tending.', source: 'Numbers 31:19–24' },
      { title: 'Don’t let others fight alone', text: 'Moses asks Reuben and Gad: “Will your brothers go to war while you sit here?” They promise to march at the front. Being part of a community means sharing the hard parts too.', source: 'Numbers 32:6, 16–18' },
      { title: 'Your actions find you', text: 'Moses warns them: if you don’t keep this promise, “know that your sin will find you.” Consequences have a way of catching up.', source: 'Numbers 32:23' },
      { title: 'Keep your word', text: '“He shall not break his word; he shall do all that came out of his mouth.” Words create obligations. What we say we’ll do, we should do.', source: 'Numbers 30:3' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 30:3', headline: 'Every word is sacred, which is exactly why the rabbis feared vows.', text: '“He shall not profane his word; he shall do all that came out of his mouth.” Rashi explains <i>lo yachel</i>: he must not make his word <i>chulin</i>, ordinary. A vow turns speech into something consecrated, like an offering. Because the stakes are so high, the rabbis grew wary of vowing at all. Rabbi Natan says one who vows is as if he built a private altar, forbidden outside the Temple, and one who keeps the vow as if he sacrificed on it (Nedarim 22a). Ecclesiastes said it first: better not to vow than to vow and not pay.', try: 'Today catch yourself before one casual promise, “I’ll definitely…,” and either make it smaller and real, or don’t make it.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 31:5', headline: 'Moses hurried to the war that would end his life; Israel refused.', text: 'God tells Moses: take vengeance on Midian, “and afterwards you will be gathered to your people.” Rashi: Moses knew his death depended on this war, and went gladly, without delay. Then the people: “a thousand from each tribe were handed over.” Why “handed over”? Rashi, from the Sifrei: once Moses had said, “a little more and they will stone me.” Now that they heard his death was tied to Midian, they refused to go, until they were handed over against their will. Forty years of complaining, and at the end they would rather not fight than lose him.', try: 'Tell someone today, while they can hear it, how much it would cost you to lose them. Don’t wait for the moment when it’s about to come true.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 31:21', headline: 'In anger, Moses forgot the law, and his student taught it.', text: 'The army returns with captives, and “Moses was angry with the officers.” What follows is among the Torah’s hardest passages: he orders the deaths of the boys and of every woman who had been with a man, since such women led Israel astray at Peor. Then the law of purifying the spoils is taught not by Moses but by Elazar. Rashi: because Moses fell into anger, he fell into error, as at the rock. Reish Lakish: a sage who gets angry loses his wisdom (Pesachim 66b). And Elazar, Rashi notes, still credits it to “what the LORD commanded Moses.”', try: 'The next time you’re angry today, make no decision and give no instruction for an hour. Write it down, then look at it again.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 31:27', headline: 'Those who stayed home shared the spoils, and gave ten times more.', text: 'The spoils are split down the middle: half to the twelve thousand who fought, half to the whole community that stayed behind. Sforno explains: Midian had wronged the whole people, so the whole people shares the victory. Then a levy goes to God, at unequal rates. The soldiers give one in five hundred; the community, one in fifty, ten times as much. Chizkuni: the fighters carried the burden, so they give less. Centuries later David made it a rule for Israel: the one who stays with the baggage shares alike with the one who goes into battle (I Samuel 30:24).', try: 'Think of who held things together while you were out chasing something, a parent, a partner, a colleague covering for you. Give them a real share of the credit today.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 31:49', headline: 'No soldier was lost, or sinned, and they still brought atonement.', text: 'The officers count their troops: “Not one man of us is missing.” Then they bring gold jewelry to God, “to atone for our souls.” Atone for what? The Talmud (Shabbat 64a) tells it: Moses asked: have you gone back to the sin of Peor? They answered: not one of us is missing, not even in deed. Then why atonement? Because we escaped the act, but not the thoughts. Soldiers in a war full of captive women, with no one watching, come back and confess what went on in their heads. That is a rare kind of honesty.', try: 'Think of a temptation you resisted recently. Instead of congratulating yourself, name honestly the part of you that wanted it, and decide what to do with that wanting.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 32:16', headline: 'They mentioned the sheep before the children, and Moses noticed.', text: 'Reuben and Gad, rich in cattle, ask to stay east of the Jordan: “We will build sheepfolds for our flocks here, and towns for our children.” Rashi catches the order. They cared more for their property than for their sons and daughters, since they put the animals first. Moses answers by turning the sentence around: “Build towns for your children and folds for your sheep.” Rashi sums it up: make the essential essential, and the secondary secondary. Nobody says out loud that they love their sheep more. It comes out in the order of a sentence.', try: 'Look at your calendar or your bank statement for this week and see what actually comes first. Move one thing so a person comes ahead of a possession.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 32:22', headline: 'The incense-makers’ brides never wore perfume.', text: 'Moses lets Reuben and Gad keep their land if they fight first, “and you will be clean before the LORD and before Israel.” Not only before God: before people too. The Talmud (Yoma 38a) gives an example. The family of Avtinas made the Temple’s incense and guarded the recipe. No bride from their house ever went out perfumed, and when they married women from elsewhere, they made them promise not to wear perfume, so no one could say they were helping themselves to the Temple’s incense. They gave up a small, innocent pleasure to keep their honesty beyond suspicion.', try: 'Pick one thing you do that is honest but could look otherwise, an expense, a private message, a favor, and make it visible today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Masei': {
    meaning: 'Journeys',
    summary: 'The Torah lists all forty-two stops on Israel’s journey from Egypt to the Jordan. God sets out the borders of the land, names the leaders who will divide it, and sets aside forty-eight cities for the Levites, including six cities of refuge where someone who killed by accident can live safely. The daughters of Tzelofchad marry within their tribe, and the book of Numbers ends on the plains of Moab.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Every stop counts', text: 'The Torah lists all forty-two stops. Rashi compares it to a king retelling a long trip with his son: “Here we slept, here we were cold, here you were sick.” Every stage of the journey, even the hard ones, is part of the story.', source: 'Numbers 33:1 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'A place for a terrible mistake', text: 'Six cities of refuge protect someone who killed by accident from revenge. The Torah separates a tragic mistake from murder, and makes a place to go on living.', source: 'Numbers 35:9–15' },
      { title: 'Don’t pollute the land', text: '“Do not defile the land you live in, in which I also dwell.” Bloodshed and injustice pollute a place. The land holds the memory of how people lived on it.', source: 'Numbers 35:33–34' },
      { title: 'Life happens on the way', text: 'The portion is called <i>Masei</i>, “journeys,” and Numbers ends not with arriving but at the edge of the land. Much of life happens on the way.', source: 'Numbers 33:1–2' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Numbers 33:1', headline: 'God recites the route like a father bringing a sick child home.', text: '“These are the journeys of the children of Israel”: forty-two stops, listed one by one. Why record them? Rashi gives two answers. First, mercy hidden in arithmetic: fourteen stops came in the first year and eight in the last, so in thirty-eight years of punishment they moved only twenty times. Second, a parable from the Midrash: a king takes his sick son far away to be healed, and on the way home he names each place: here we slept, here you were cold, here your head hurt. The list is not bookkeeping. It is a father remembering.', try: 'Write down the stops of one hard stretch of your life, the places, the people, the worst night. Then read it back the way someone who loves you would.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 33:40', headline: 'When Aaron died, his enemies saw what he had been holding back.', text: 'In the middle of the itinerary comes a date: Aaron dies on Mount Hor on the first of Av, at a hundred twenty-three. The next verse repeats a battle already told: “The Canaanite king of Arad heard.” Why repeat it? Rashi, from the Talmud (Rosh Hashanah 3a): what he heard was that Aaron had died and the clouds of glory that sheltered Israel were gone, and he took it as license to attack. The people wept for Aaron for thirty days. But it was the enemy who first understood what his presence had been holding back.', try: 'Ask yourself whose shelter you are: someone younger, weaker, or newer than you. Show up for them today in a way they will notice.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 33:53', headline: 'Ramban made settling the land a commandment, then went there himself.', text: '“Take possession of the land and settle in it.” Ramban reads this as a full commandment, binding in every generation: not to reject God’s gift. From this verse he explains the Talmud’s hard ruling that a husband or wife who refuses to move to the land can be compelled, or divorced (Ketubot 110b). He lived it. Forced out of Spain after defending Judaism in a public disputation, he reached Jerusalem in 1267, an old man, found it nearly empty of Jews, and set up a synagogue. A commentary is easier to write than to live.', try: 'Name one value you’ve argued for but never paid a price for. Today take one concrete step toward living it that actually costs you something.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 34:19', headline: 'Forty-five years later, Caleb asked for the mountain of the giants.', text: 'God names the leaders who will divide the land, one per tribe: “For the tribe of Judah, Caleb son of Yefuneh.” Of the twelve scouts, only he is on this list too. Rashi says that on the scouting trip Caleb alone went to Hebron, the giants’ city, to pray at the patriarchs’ graves that he not be swept along by the others. Years later, at eighty-five, he tells Joshua: I am as strong as when Moses sent me; give me this hill country, where the giants live (Joshua 14:10–12). He receives Hebron.', try: 'Name the task you’ve avoided longest because it scares you. Today ask for it, out loud, from the person who can give it to you.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 35:6', headline: 'Jacob cursed Levi to be scattered, and the curse became a calling.', text: 'The Levites get no territory, only forty-eight cities spread through every tribe. Dying, Jacob had cursed Levi and Simeon for their violence at Shechem: “I will divide them in Jacob and scatter them in Israel.” Rashi says how it came true: Simeon’s descendants became poor scribes and teachers, wandering for a living, and Levi went from threshing floor to threshing floor for tithes, scattered with more dignity. Here the scattering becomes a structure: Levites in every corner, and among their cities, six cities of refuge. The tribe whose ancestor killed in rage now shelters those who killed by accident.', try: 'Take one trait you’ve been told is your flaw, intensity, restlessness, stubbornness, and today put it to work for someone else.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 35:25', headline: 'The killer’s exile ended with the High Priest, who shared the blame.', text: 'Whoever kills by accident must live in a city of refuge “until the death of the High Priest.” Why him? Rashi gives a hard answer, from the Talmud (Makkot 11a): the High Priest should have prayed that no such disaster happen in Israel in his days. So the High Priests’ mothers fed and clothed the exiles, the Mishnah says, so they wouldn’t pray for their sons to die. The Talmud even tells that Elijah shunned Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi for three days because a lion killed a man near his town. Leaders answer for what they didn’t pray for.', try: 'Think of something bad that happened near you this year that you decided wasn’t your business. Today do one thing about it, or at least pray about it by name.' },
      { verse: 'Numbers 36:6', headline: 'A rule keeping the tribes apart ended in a festival of matchmaking.', text: 'The book closes on a worry: if Tzelofchad’s daughters marry outside their tribe, their land will pass out of it. So Moses rules: “Let them marry whom they please, only within their father’s tribe.” The Talmud (Taanit 30b) says the rule was read as binding on that generation only, and the day it was lifted, the fifteenth of Av, became a festival. The Mishnah describes it: the daughters of Jerusalem went out in borrowed white clothes to dance in the vineyards, and young men came to choose a bride. Numbers ends with a restriction; the rabbis remembered its undoing.', try: 'Think of a barrier that once made sense in your life, about who you date, whom you talk to, where you go. Ask whether it belonged to an earlier you, and if so, lift it today.' },
    ],
  },
});
